{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he shall sprinkle upon him who is to be cleansed of the tzara'at, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>of the tzara'at</b> when it is him the verse is speaking of. And perhaps it is because since this sprinkling does not purify him definitively, but [it is] like the day a zavah ceases from the flow of her blood, and he still needs a counting of seven days according to the law of the tum'ah of a zav, and you will find that all the days of his counting he is still a father of tum'ah — therefore it said <b>the one to be cleansed of the tzara'at</b>, meaning, of the body of the tzara'at and not of the tum'ah of the tzara'at. And therefore it said at the end: ״וְהָיָה בַיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי וְגוֹ׳ וְכִבֶּס אֶת בְּגָדָיו״ — this teaches you that during the days of his counting he would render garments impure, and behold he is a father of tum'ah, and therefore it informed you that he is not purified except from the body of the tzara'at, and after he is purified from the tzara'at he must remain in his tum'ah seven days." "<b>And in the Torah of</b> the priests they expounded: Why was it necessary to say ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת״ since it is him the verse is speaking of? And they said that it was necessary to say ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת״ to exclude a tevel met (one impure through corpse contact), that he does not require seven sprinklings, because I would reason a kal va-chomer from the metzora, who does not require three and seven, and even so requires seven sprinklings — a tevel met, who does require three and seven, is it not logical that he should require seven sprinklings? Scripture says: ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת״ — and not from the tum'ah of a corpse. And they further expounded at the end of that same baraita: ״וְטִהֲרוֹ״ — what was the necessity of saying ״וְטִהֲרוֹ״? For it comes to exclude the metzora from requiring three and seven, for without this I would reason a kal va-chomer from a tevel met, who does not require seven and even so requires three and seven — a metzora, who requires seven, is it not logical that he should require three and seven? Scripture says: ״וְטִהֲרוֹ״, meaning, with this it suffices to purify him — end of quotation." "And the difficulty for me is: after the verse said ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת״ and we expounded it to exclude the tum'ah of a corpse, then the exclusion is justified only insofar as it would come by logical reasoning, and this reasoning applies only if we say that the metzora does not have three and seven — for in that case a kal va-chomer would arise: and what of the metzora, etc., as stated in the words of the baraita. If so, by saying ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת״ the verse established this premise and sealed it, that the metzora does not have three and seven, and from this point why was it necessary to say ״וְטִהֲרוֹ״ to exclude him from three and seven? For it was already necessitated by the verse to say that he does not have three and seven, from the statement ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת.״ Or, he should have said ״וְטִהֲרוֹ״ — that the metzora does not need three and seven — and it would not have been necessary to say ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת,״ and I would know that a tevel met does not need seven sprinklings from the fact that it was necessary to say ״וְטִהֲרוֹ״ to negate the kal va-chomer that comes from a tevel met regarding the sprinkling of three and seven. And a kal va-chomer would not arise unless you say that there is only one element in the melammed (source category), and understand this." "<b>And it appears</b> to explain, that since we see that HaShem commanded regarding the tum'ah of a corpse the sprinkling of three and seven (Numbers 19:18–19) and did not mention how many sprinklings, and mentioned regarding the tum'ah of tzara'at seven times and did not mention three and seven — there is reason to say that the purification of the greater number of sprinklings is a greater purification than the sprinkling of three and seven, because they are seven times, whereas the sprinklings of three and seven are only two occasions. And accordingly, the verse required a greater arrangement for the purification of the metzora than the arrangement of the purification of corpse tum'ah. And there is reason to say that the purification by the counting of days is greater, that one day alone would not suffice to purify — and if so, what we find that HaShem required for a tevel met three and seven is a greater escalation than the sprinkling of seven times. And these two lines of reasoning will divide among the opinions of the Sages, and there will be a Sage who holds that what is stated regarding the purification of the metzora is greater than what is stated regarding the purification of a tevel met, and there will be a Sage who holds the opposite. Now, for the one who holds that the purification of three and seven is greater, he will come to reason a kal va-chomer from the metzora to require seven times for a tevel met. And for the one who holds that the purification of seven times is more stringent, he will derive for the metzora three and seven from a tevel met by a kal va-chomer. And from this point the verse is obligated to state both exclusions, to remove the error of every possible line of reasoning. And if it had said only one — for example, if it said ״מִן הַצָּרַעַת״ to exclude a tevel met that he does not need seven times — the reasoning of the one who argues would come and say that by this the verse removed the error on its opposing side and justified saying that the sprinkling of seven times is greater and it applies to the metzora and not to a tevel met, and I in turn reason to bring upon him three and seven from a tevel met, and likewise if it had said ״וְטִהֲרוֹ״ etc." "And so that your mind does not recoil from this path, to say how the verse could come to exclude a line of reasoning that is not true — why, you will find that they said (Berakhot 47a) in several places: ״עָלֶיךָ אָמַר קְרָא״, meaning, even though his opinion is not so, behold the Tanna will expound the verse that addresses a line of reasoning with which his own mind does not agree, that the verse is concerned for one who would reason thus, and understand this:" "<b>And I saw</b> in the wondrous Rav, the RY Rozanis, may his merit protect us, in the honored book Mishneh LaMelekh (Hilkhot Ḥametz u-Matzah, chapter 6, halakhah 1), that he brought a baraita similar to what is before us. For it is taught in Torat Kohanim, chapter 11, and this is their language: ״הַזֶּה חַג הַמַּצּוֹת״ — this day requires matzah, and the festival of Sukkot does not require matzah, when logic would say: if this one, which does not require sukkan, requires matzah, then this one, which does require sukkan — is it not logical that it should require matzah? Scripture therefore says: ״הַזֶּה חַג הַמַּצּוֹת״ — it requires matzah, etc. And there in chapter 14 it is taught, and this is their language: ״הַזֶּה חַג הַסֻּכּוֹת״ — this one requires sukkah, and the festival of Matzot does not require sukkah, when logic would say: if this one, which does not require matzah, requires sukkah, then the festival of Matzot, which does require matzah — is it not logical that it should require sukkah? Scripture therefore says: ״הַזֶּה״ — this one requires sukkah and not etc. And the Rav himself raised the very difficulties that we wrote regarding the baraitot of the purification of the metzora, so remove from the book of the Rav whatever he answered on this matter. And his words, of blessed memory, are somewhat forced, especially according to the school of the poskim (Rambam and others) who hold that a Torah-level doubt is permitted by Torah law — the words of the Rav, peace be upon him, will not stand up under scrutiny regarding this." "And in my opinion the baraitot are explained by the very approach that we explained above, namely that on the one hand there is a greater publicity of the miracle of the Exodus from Egypt in the sukkah in one respect than the publicity of the miracle that exists in the eating of matzah, for a person eats his bread as matzah many times, whereas the act of building a sukkah has great publicity. And in Passover too there is another respect, in that the miracle is more publicized in its appointed time and season, which is on the fifteenth of Nisan. Now a sage may come who will have reason to magnify the publicity of the miracle of sukkah, which is greater than the publicity of eating matzah, and he will argue a fortiori that sukkah should require matzah, and he will say: ״And if the festival of Matzot, in which HaShem did not require so much publicity, nevertheless obligated matzah since it is something necessary in any case — sukkah, which we see has great publicity, is it not logical that it requires matzah?״ Scripture therefore says: ״הַזֶּה חַג הַמַּצּוֹת.״ And then another line of reasoning may come and argue the opposite, and say that its hand has prevailed, and it rejects the opposing reasoning from the fact that Scripture limited sukkah in that it has no matzah, and the essence of the publicity is the matzah, and it will argue to obligate Passover with sukkah by a fortiori reasoning from sukkah, in which HaShem did not require matzah and even so required sukkah — Scripture therefore says: ״[הַזֶּה] חַג הַסֻּכּוֹת.״ And you should know that the Tanna who expounds the limiting clauses does so for both lines of reasoning, by noting in the first a fortiori argument that the Tanna makes, how he produced an a fortiori argument from his mouth while he still does not know whether it requires only one or two — and what I have explained will fit correctly. And as for what the great Rav, author of Mishneh LaMelekh, wrote — that we would not know in which case the a fortiori argument is stated, and it would suffice for us to say that the festival of Matzot requires sukkah and the festival of Sukkot requires matzah — this far, his words, may his memory be for a blessing, are not compelling. For since you cannot argue an a fortiori argument on account of your inability to say ״and if this one requires only this,״ for who told you that it requires only this? Perhaps you will argue the a fortiori the other way and it will require both. And likewise on the other side I cannot construct the a fortiori from the reason itself, and consequently each one would stand as it is and you would not argue any a fortiori at all — unless you say that there is one face to argue an a fortiori from one side and there is another face to argue an a fortiori from the other side, and Scripture was careful about both sides. And that is the approach I have explained."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {5 "הר״י רוזאנישׁ is transliterated as written; the identity is uncertain. The phrase דִּרְשׁוּ מֵעַל סֵפֶר הָרַב is read as 'remove from the book of the Rav,' i.e., disregard the Rav's proposed answer."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:13.126977744Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}